# 1. PURPOSE AND NON-NEGOTIABLE SCOPE

This document defines the complete shared foundation that must be built before any business Module is started.

The 50 future business Modules are intentionally excluded. No Module-specific workflow, table, page, report, permission, route or API endpoint is to be invented in this phase.

The Core must operate when the Modules directory is empty. It must provide secure user accounts, centralized authentication, roles, permissions, shared UI, database services, internal service contracts and an authenticated external API gateway.

The future Modules will not all behave the same way. Each will own its own business logic, data model, workflow, screens and rules. The Core must provide common infrastructure without forcing all Modules into one generic workflow.

Read the complete current document and inspect the actual repository before implementation. This document expands Core and User Management. It does not silently replace or weaken the Master Documentation.

If two specifications conflict, record the exact clauses and request the Project Owner's decision. Do not silently choose the easier option.

## 1.1 Included

- Application bootstrap, routing and request lifecycle.
- Configuration, environment separation and secret handling.
- One central SQLite database and PDO connection manager.
- Core schema and migration infrastructure.
- Authentication, account lifecycle and sessions.
- User administration and profile management.
- Roles, permissions and server-side authorization.
- Shared layout, design system and navigation infrastructure.
- Settings, preferences and administration.
- Internal API registry for future in-process Module communication.
- External versioned HTTP API for other applications.
- API clients, credentials, scopes, rate limits and audit.
- Module registry infrastructure only; no business Modules.
- Audit, logs, notifications and file infrastructure.
- Backup, restore, diagnostics and deployment.
- Automated, integration, security and acceptance testing.

## 1.2 Excluded

- All 50 business Modules and their individual features.
- Module-specific database tables.
- Module-specific approval workflows.
- Module-specific reports or dashboards.
- Module-specific public API endpoints.
- Fake Module screens or demo business records.
- Microservices or multiple database files.
- Direct database access by browser JavaScript or external applications.

## 1.3 Mandatory Architecture

- One deployable PHP application.
- Modular Monolith.
- One central SQLite database file.
- Central PDO SQLite connection management.
- PHP-only database access.
- Browser-to-server communication through HTTP endpoints.
- Shared authentication and authorization.
- Internal API for in-process service communication.
