# 3. PRODUCT MODEL AND IDENTITY CONCEPTS

The system must distinguish a human person, login account, profile, role, permission, session, API client and API credential.

A person may exist without a login account.

A login account is the credential-bearing identity used to authenticate.

A profile contains the minimum display and preference information needed by the Core.

A future employee or people-related Module may own employment details. The Core must not become a substitute employee-management Module.

A role is a named collection of permissions.

A permission is a specific capability.

A session is an authenticated interaction context.

An API client is a software identity used by another application.

An API credential proves the API client's identity but does not grant unrestricted access.

An audit actor identifies the human user or service client responsible for an action.

Do not confuse these entities in database design, code or UI labels.

