# 6. CORE SERVICE CONTRACTS

Every Core service must have one responsibility, a documented public interface, explicit dependencies, validated inputs, predictable outputs, stable errors, authorization rules, logging rules and tests.

Use constructor injection or a lightweight service registry where practical.

Avoid hidden global mutable state and circular dependencies.

A service must not silently fall back to a fake implementation in production.

A service failure must fail safely.

Public interfaces must be stable enough for future Modules to consume.

Private implementation details must remain private.

---

# 7. HTTP REQUEST, ROUTER AND RESPONSE

The Request abstraction must expose method, normalized path, query parameters, parsed body, headers, content type, request ID and authenticated context where available.

Every input source is untrusted: query strings, path parameters, form fields, JSON, headers, cookies, uploads and Internal API payloads.

The router must use explicit method/path/handler/middleware definitions.

Duplicate route names must be rejected.

Unknown routes return 404.

Unsupported methods return 405 where appropriate.

Protected routes require authentication and server-side authorization.

Browser state-changing requests require CSRF protection.

API routes use API authentication rather than relying on browser session cookies.

The Response abstraction controls status, headers, content type and body.

HTML, JSON, redirects, downloads and empty responses must be handled consistently.

An API caller must never receive an HTML login page with HTTP 200 when JSON was expected.

