# 8. CENTRAL DATABASE ARCHITECTURE

## 8.1 One Database

All application data will use one central SQLite database file.

All database access goes through the Core Database Manager.

No Module creates another database file.

No external application receives database credentials.

No browser code accesses SQLite.

## 8.2 Connection Manager

Use PDO SQLite with exception error handling and prepared statements.

Enable foreign key enforcement.

Configure and verify a documented busy timeout.

Configure WAL mode where the filesystem and hosting environment support it.

Use one managed connection per PHP request lifecycle.

Do not create unmanaged connections inside services.

Keep write transactions short.

SQLite permits concurrent readers but serializes writers. The application must not promise unrestricted concurrent writes.

## 8.3 Core Table Ownership

Core-owned tables use a reserved prefix such as `core_`.

The final prefix must be registered and protected.

Future Module prefixes must be separate.

Core services own Core tables.

Future Modules own their own tables.

No Module may alter another Module's tables.

Cross-boundary data access uses documented APIs.

## 8.4 Migration Engine

Migrations require unique immutable identifiers, deterministic order, applied-history tracking, duplicate prevention, failure reporting and recovery instructions.

Destructive changes require backup and explicit approval.

Applied migration files must not be edited to rewrite history.

Corrections use new migrations.

Migrations run through an explicit CLI or authorized maintenance operation, not on every page request.

Test migrations on an empty database and a representative copy.

## 8.5 Database Integrity and Performance

Use primary keys, foreign keys, appropriate unique constraints, documented status values and consistent UTC timestamps.

Index actual query patterns.

Avoid duplicate indexes.

Select required columns rather than `SELECT *`.

Paginate large lists.

Use allowlists for sort columns.

Bind all data values.

Review query plans for high-volume paths.

Do not silently discard writes after `SQLITE_BUSY` or `SQLITE_LOCKED`.

Use bounded retries only where safe.

Never manually delete SQLite WAL/SHM files while the database is active.

Back up using a SQLite-consistent procedure.

