# 9. USER MANAGEMENT

## 9.1 Account Lifecycle

Support the account states required by the approved policy: pending, active, temporarily locked, suspended, disabled and optionally archived.

Allowed state transitions must be documented.

A browser-submitted status value cannot activate or elevate an account.

Every privileged status change must be authorized and audited.

Suspension and temporary lockout are different states.

Disabling an account must not delete historical audit records.

## 9.2 User Creation

Only authorized administrators can create accounts.

Validate all required identity fields.

Normalize login identifiers consistently.

Enforce uniqueness in both application validation and database constraints.

Do not reveal whether an account exists through public login or recovery messages.

Assign only roles the acting administrator is authorized to grant.

Use a secure activation or password setup process.

Never send a plaintext password through an insecure channel.

Record account creation in the audit trail.

## 9.3 User Profile

Core profile fields may include display name, username, email, phone, profile-photo reference, language, time zone, date format, theme preference, status and timestamps.

Store only necessary information.

Validate every field.

A user may edit only fields permitted by policy.

Sensitive identity changes may require re-verification or administrator approval.

Profile editing must not implicitly modify roles or permissions.

## 9.4 User Listing

Provide paginated user listing.

Support authorized search and filters.

Support allowlisted sorting.

Display account state clearly.

Do not load every account into the browser.

Never return password hashes, reset tokens, session secrets or API credentials.

## 9.5 User Detail

Show permitted profile fields, roles, account status and timestamps.

Show recent security events only to authorized viewers.

Do not expose raw session identifiers or secrets.

Separate ordinary profile editing from privileged account actions.

## 9.6 Update and Deactivation

Use explicit field allowlists to prevent mass assignment.

Reload the target account before applying sensitive changes.

Check the acting user's authority over the target.

Detect stale updates where overwriting concurrent changes would be harmful.

Disabling an account must invalidate sessions according to policy.

Review API credentials associated with the account.

Preserve historical attribution.

Do not delete business data when a login account is disabled.

## 9.7 Reactivation and Deletion

Reactivation requires explicit authority.

Review roles before reactivation.

Do not automatically restore old sessions or revoked API credentials.

Permanent deletion is not the default.

Hard deletion requires a documented retention/privacy process and must preserve referential integrity.

