# 10. AUTHENTICATION AND PASSWORD SECURITY

Use `password_hash()`, `password_verify()` and `password_needs_rehash()`.

Use Argon2id where supported and appropriately configured; otherwise use PHP's secure default.

Never store plaintext passwords.

Never log passwords.

Never return hashes in HTML or JSON.

Define password length and weak-password policy with the Project Owner.

Allow password managers and paste operations.

The login flow validates request format, applies throttling, normalizes the identifier, verifies the password, checks account status, regenerates the session ID, establishes identity and records the event.

Public errors should be generic to reduce account enumeration.

Failed attempts must be rate limited without enabling trivial denial of service against a victim account.

Password change should verify the current password for normal self-service changes.

Password recovery tokens must be random, high entropy, single-use, expiring and stored as hashes.

If email delivery is not configured, do not pretend an email was sent.

Administrator-assisted resets must be authorized and audited.

MFA may be added later, but must not be claimed as implemented until enrollment, verification, recovery and enforcement work end to end.

---

# 11. SESSION SECURITY

Use server-side sessions.

Regenerate session IDs after login and privilege changes.

Use HttpOnly cookies.

Use Secure cookies in HTTPS production.

Set a documented SameSite policy.

Define idle timeout and absolute lifetime separately.

Invalidate the server-side session on logout.

Revoke sessions after suspension or password reset according to policy.

Allow authorized session revocation.

Do not rely only on deleting a browser cookie.

Do not store passwords or API secrets in session data.

Remember-me is optional; if implemented, use random hashed, expiring, revocable and rotated tokens.

---

# 12. ROLES, PERMISSIONS AND AUTHORIZATION

Use role-based access control with default deny.

A role contains permissions.

A permission has a stable identifier and description.

Use names such as `core.users.view`, `core.users.create`, `core.roles.manage` and `core.api_clients.manage`.

Do not reuse a permission identifier for a different meaning.

Role creation, assignment and permission changes require authorization and audit.

Prevent self-escalation.

Prevent accidental removal of the last administrator.

Every protected backend action checks permission.

Hiding a button or menu item is not a security control.

Unknown permissions are denied.

A database or permission-service failure must not result in allow-all behavior.

If resource-level restrictions are later needed, define explicit policies and tests; do not hide them in templates.

Permission caches are optional and must be invalidated after role, permission or account-state changes.

