# 15. EXTERNAL API — OTHER APPLICATION INTEGRATION

## 15.1 Purpose and Boundary

Other applications must be able to consume approved application data through a documented HTTP API.

External applications must never open the SQLite file or receive database credentials.

The External API is a stable contract, not a raw database proxy.

The Core phase builds the gateway, authentication, client management, scopes, validation, versioning, rate limits, logging and documentation.

No Module-specific endpoint is created until that Module's data and business rules are specified.

## 15.2 Internal API Versus External API

Internal API: in-process PHP calls between Core and future Modules, with trusted caller context.

External API: HTTPS HTTP endpoints for other applications, with explicit API credentials, scopes, versioning, request validation, JSON responses, rate limits and public contract documentation.

An Internal API action never becomes public automatically.

An external endpoint must not expose an internal method without a security and contract review.

## 15.3 Versioned Base Path

Use a versioned path such as `/api/v1/`.

Centralize the base path.

Use HTTPS in production.

Reject unsupported versions clearly.

Do not silently reinterpret old requests using incompatible new schemas.

Breaking changes require a new major version or an explicitly approved versioning policy.

## 15.4 API Authentication

The first release may use random API tokens with hashed storage.

Generate tokens with a cryptographically secure random generator.

Show plaintext only once at creation.

Store only a suitable one-way hash.

Send credentials in the Authorization header.

Never put tokens in query strings.

Never log tokens.

Support expiry, rotation, disablement and revocation.

OAuth 2.0 may be considered later if delegated authorization is genuinely required. Do not claim OAuth support until the complete flow is implemented and tested.

## 15.5 API Client Registry

Each client has stable ID, display name, purpose, owner/contact, status, created/updated timestamps, optional expiry and assigned scopes.

Creation, change, rotation and revocation are audited.

A client is a software identity, not a human login account.

## 15.6 API Scopes

Scopes must be narrow and explicit.

Separate read and write access.

Use separate scopes for sensitive exports and administrative operations.

Unknown scopes are rejected.

Every endpoint checks the required scope.

A valid token without the required scope receives 403.

## 15.7 Request and Response

Validate method, content type, body size, JSON syntax, required fields, types, lengths, allowed values and scope.

Use a consistent JSON envelope.

Do not return stack traces, SQL statements, filesystem paths, hashes, reset tokens or internal-only fields.

Use stable error codes and include a request ID.

Dates and identifiers must use documented formats.

## 15.8 Pagination and Filtering

Every collection endpoint is bounded.

Define default and maximum page sizes.

Use cursor pagination where appropriate.

Allowlist sort fields and direction.

Document filters and operators.

Bind all filter values.

Do not allow arbitrary SQL expressions.

Do not return records outside the client's authorized scope.

## 15.9 Rate Limiting and Idempotency

Apply rate limits per credential and, where appropriate, IP.

Return HTTP 429 when exceeded.

Provide retry guidance where practical.

State-changing operations that may be retried should support idempotency keys.

Scope idempotency keys to client and operation.

Reject reuse of the same key with a different payload.

Bound idempotency record retention.

## 15.10 CORS and Transport

CORS is only for browser cross-origin access; it is not authentication.

Allowlist origins.

Do not combine wildcard origins with credentialed requests.

Use HTTPS.

Trust forwarded headers only from configured trusted proxies.

Control caching of sensitive responses.

## 15.11 API Documentation

Maintain accurate structured documentation, preferably OpenAPI if maintenance cost is acceptable.

Document base URL, version, authentication, scopes, endpoints, methods, headers, parameters, request schema, response schema, errors, pagination, rate limits, idempotency, examples, deprecation and support contact.

Documentation must describe only endpoints that exist.

## 15.12 API Auditing

Record client ID, endpoint, method, timestamp, status, latency and request ID where appropriate.

Do not log bearer tokens or sensitive payloads.

Credential lifecycle actions must be audited.

Support investigation of suspicious usage.

