# 16. MODULE REGISTRY FOUNDATION — WITHOUT MODULE CREATION

The Core may provide metadata infrastructure for future Modules.

This does not authorize creating any business Module.

Registry metadata may include stable ID, display name, version, description, entry route, navigation metadata, required Core version, dependencies, permissions, API actions, migration identifiers and enabled state.

Validate metadata.

Reject duplicate IDs.

Constrain paths to approved locations.

Do not include arbitrary PHP files based on directory names.

Disabled Modules must not execute routes or API handlers.

Disabling a Module must not delete its data.

The application must start when no business Module is installed.

---

# 17. AUDIT, LOGS AND SECURITY EVENTS

Audit events record actor, action, target, time, outcome, request ID and source channel.

Audit records are not ordinary debug logs.

Ordinary users must not rewrite historical audit records.

Audit user creation, sensitive profile changes, role assignments, permission changes, account status changes, session revocation, API client lifecycle, security settings, migrations, backups and restores.

Never record passwords, tokens or reset secrets.

Application logs use levels such as debug, info, warning, error and critical.

Logs must be private, bounded/rotated and correlated with request IDs.

Security monitoring may identify repeated login failures, invalid token use, permission denial spikes and suspicious API traffic.

Do not automatically suspend accounts based only on noisy signals without a reviewed policy.

---

# 18. NOTIFICATIONS, FILES AND SHARED DATA SERVICES

Notifications have recipient, type, safe content, read state and timestamps.

Notification creation is not proof of email/SMS delivery.

External delivery requires configured and approved providers.

Files are stored outside the public web root.

Store file metadata in the database and binary files in controlled storage.

Generate server-side filenames.

Validate actual content, MIME type and size.

Do not trust file extensions or browser MIME headers.

Disallow executable uploads.

Private downloads require authorization.

Export files are private, bounded and expire.

CSV exports must prevent spreadsheet formula injection.

Imports require header validation, row-level errors, duplicate policy and transaction strategy.

Search must enforce authorization and paginate results.

Cache is optional; permission-sensitive cache keys and invalidation must be correct.

---

# 19. BACKGROUND TASKS AND WEBHOOKS

A background worker is not required unless a real use case justifies it.

A cron-triggered PHP command may be more suitable for modest hosting.

Tasks need stable IDs, schedules, timeouts, retries, locks, status and bounded work.

Prevent duplicate concurrent execution unless designed for it.

Webhooks are optional outbound HTTP notifications.

Use an outbox approach where reliable delivery is required.

Validate HTTPS destinations and defend against SSRF.

Sign webhook payloads.

Include event IDs for deduplication.

Bound retries and move repeated failures to a failed/dead-letter state.

---

# 20. SECURITY ENGINEERING

Use prepared statements.

Escape output by context.

Validate all inputs.

Enforce authorization server-side.

Use CSRF protection for browser-session state changes.

Regenerate sessions after login.

Use HTTPS in production.

Keep private files outside public access.

Keep secrets out of Git.

Disable verbose production errors.

Prevent path traversal.

Review URL fetching for SSRF.

Validate file uploads.

Minimize personal data.

Define retention and deletion policies.

Maintain an incident response procedure for credential exposure, session revocation, evidence preservation and recovery.

Security controls must be tested, not merely documented.

---

# 21. HEALTH, DIAGNOSTICS AND OPERATIONS

A public health endpoint must reveal minimal information.

An authenticated diagnostic screen may show application version, PHP version, required extension status, database connectivity, schema version, storage writability and service status.

Mask secrets.

Do not reveal private paths.

Health checks must not run expensive maintenance.

Maintenance mode must be auditable.

Operational logs must be private.

---

# 22. BACKUP, RESTORE AND DISASTER RECOVERY

Define backup frequency, retention, destination, access control, encryption and recovery objectives.

A live SQLite database must be backed up consistently with WAL state.

Use SQLite backup facilities or another verified consistent procedure.

A complete application backup may include database, required private files, schema/migration version and deployment metadata.

A backup is not valid merely because a file exists.

Verify backup integrity.

Test restore in isolation.

After restore, check integrity, schema, file permissions, login and core workflows.

Record restore activity.

Do not overwrite the only live copy during recovery.

---

# 23. DEPLOYMENT AND RELEASE

Point the web root to `Public/` where possible.

Keep Database, Config and Storage outside public access.

Use HTTPS.

Use least-privilege filesystem permissions.

Disable debug mode.

Remove/disable installer access after setup.

Change temporary credentials.

Before updates, identify code version, migrations, config changes, backup and recovery plan.

Use Git for source control.

Do not commit live database data, secrets, logs or private uploads.

Use meaningful commits and release tags.

---

# 24. TESTING AND QUALITY ASSURANCE

Use isolated test databases.

Never run destructive tests against production by default.

Unit-test validation, routing, response construction, permission evaluation and API schema checks.

Integration-test real PDO SQLite behavior, migrations, transactions, users, sessions, roles, audit and API credentials.

Security-test SQL injection, XSS, CSRF, session fixation, privilege escalation, brute force, token leakage, path traversal, SSRF and upload validation where applicable.

Acceptance-test first admin setup, login, logout, user creation, role assignment, permission denial, account disabling, session revocation, API client creation, API authentication, scope denial, credential revocation, audit and backup restore.

Browser-test desktop and mobile, keyboard navigation, forms, empty states, errors, session expiry and permission-dependent navigation.

Record environment, command/procedure, expected result, actual result and pass/fail.

Do not delete a failing test to make the suite green.

---

# 25. PERFORMANCE AND SQLITE CONCURRENCY

Measure on the target hosting environment.

Keep requests efficient.

Avoid N+1 queries.

Avoid unbounded lists.

Use indexes based on actual queries.

Keep SQLite write transactions short.

Do not promise simultaneous independent writes.

Handle busy/locked errors safely.

Use bounded retries only for safe operations.

Do not silently discard writes.

Test realistic data volume and concurrent reads/writes.

Record CPU, memory and response time.

